You are reverse-engineering a compiled C++ binary as a BLACK BOX. The binary is at /workspace/dataset/test15_executable.

Invocation contract: it takes EXACTLY 6 positional args: scheme host port path query fragment.
It prints 8 lines to stdout: scheme(), host(), port(), path(), query(), fragment(), to_string(), is_valid().

RULES OF ENGAGEMENT:
- You may ONLY run the binary and observe stdout/stderr/exit code. There is NO C++ source available; do not look for any.
- ALWAYS quote every argument in the shell, including empty ones: /workspace/dataset/test15_executable "http" "example.com" "80" "/" "" ""
- Make output unambiguous. Use a helper that numbers the lines and marks empties, e.g.:
    p(){ printf '### IN: [%s]\n' "$*"; /workspace/dataset/test15_executable "$@" >/tmp/o 2>/tmp/e; printf '  exit=%d\n' $?; nl -ba -s': ' /tmp/o | sed 's/$/<END>/'; if [ -s /tmp/e ]; then echo "  STDERR: $(cat /tmp/e)"; fi; }
  Then call p with 6 quoted args. Beware: trailing empty args are easy to lose — verify your helper really passes 6.
- Run MANY cases (30+ minimum). Batch them into a few Bash calls; do not do one call per case.
- Be exhaustive about the boundary cases in your assigned dimension.
- Do not guess. Every rule you report must be backed by an observed input/output pair you actually ran.

Baseline facts already established (re-verify if relevant to you):
- ("http","example.com","80","/","","")  -> lines: http / example.com / 80 / "/" / "" / "" / "http://example.com:80/" / 1
- ("http","example.com","-1","/","","") -> to_string is "http://example.com/"  (port omitted), port() prints -1
- ("http","example.com","0","/","","")  -> to_string is "http://example.com:0/"  (port 0 IS included)
- ("http","","80","/","","")            -> to_string is "http://:80/", is_valid=1
- ("","example.com","80","/","","")     -> to_string is "/" (scheme+authority both vanish), is_valid=1
- ("","","0","","","")                  -> to_string is "", is_valid=0

Report rules as precise, implementable statements (pseudocode-level), each with the concrete evidence line.


DIMENSION UNDER REVIEW: cross-field-interactions

A previous investigator probed this dimension and inferred the following rules. Your job is ADVERSARIAL: actively try to FALSIFY each rule by finding inputs where it predicts the wrong output. For every rule, design at least 2 new inputs (not already tested) that would expose the rule as wrong if it is wrong, run them, and compare prediction vs reality. Pay special attention to off-by-one boundaries, empty strings, sign boundaries, and whether a rule that was only tested with one other field set still holds when that field is empty.

RULES TO ATTACK:
[
  {
    "rule": "to_string() is a pure concatenation with NO re-parsing:  s = scheme.empty() ? \"\" : (scheme + \"://\" + host + (port>=0 ? \":\"+itoa(port) : \"\"));  s += path;  if(!query.empty()) s += \"?\"+query;  if(!fragment.empty()) s += \"#\"+fragment;  return s;",
    "evidence": "(\"https\",\"www.google.com\",\"443\",\"/search\",\"q=test\",\"results\") -> line7 = \"https://www.google.com:443/search?q=test#results\"; (\"http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"\",\"q=1\",\"f\") -> line7 = \"http://h:80?q=1#f\"; (\"http\",\"h\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"f\") -> line7 = \"http://h#f\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "The ENTIRE authority (host AND the \":port\" piece) is emitted only when scheme is non-empty. If scheme==\"\", both host and port vanish from to_string() even though host()/port() still report them. Full 2x2x2 truth table confirmed.",
    "evidence": "(\"\",\"hh\",\"80\",\"/p\",\"\",\"\") -> lines: 2=\"hh\", 3=\"80\", but line7 = \"/p\"; (\"\",\"hh\",\"80\",\"\",\"q\",\"f\") -> line7 = \"?q#f\"; contrast (\"http\",\"hh\",\"80\",\"/p\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 = \"http://hh:80/p\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "The \"://\" separator is emitted unconditionally after a non-empty scheme, even when host is empty and port<0 — producing \"scheme://\" with nothing after it (and \"scheme:///path\" when only path is set).",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 = \"http://\", line8=0; (\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"/p\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 = \"http:///p\"; (\"file\",\"\",\"-1\",\"/etc/hosts\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 = \"file:///etc/hosts\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "from_components does NOT re-split the PATH argument on '?' or '#'. Embedded '?'/'#' stay inside path(); query() and fragment() remain exactly as passed. This makes to_string() ambiguous/lossy.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/p?x=1#f\",\"\",\"\") -> 4=\"/p?x=1#f\", 5=\"\"<END>, 6=\"\"<END>, 7=\"http://h:80/p?x=1#f\"; (\"http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/a?b#c\",\"d?e#f\",\"g?h#i\") -> 4=\"/a?b#c\", 5=\"d?e#f\", 6=\"g?h#i\", 7=\"http://h:80/a?b#c?d?e#f#g?h#i\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "A ':'+digits suffix embedded in HOST is NOT split into the port and does not override argv[3]; both appear, yielding a double colon-port in to_string().",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"h:9090\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> 2=\"h:9090\", 3=\"80\", 7=\"http://h:9090:80/\"; (\"http\",\"[::1]:80\",\"-1\",\"/x\",\"\",\"\") -> 2=\"[::1]:80\", 3=\"-1\", 7=\"http://[::1]:80/x\" (indistinguishable from a real port)"
  },
  {
    "rule": "\"user:pass@\" in HOST is NOT extracted as userinfo; the whole string is stored as host() verbatim and pasted before the \":port\".",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"u:p@h\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> 2=\"u:p@h\", 7=\"http://u:p@h:80/\", 8=1"
  },
  {
    "rule": "A full URL passed as the SCHEME argument is NOT parsed. It is only ASCII-lowercased and then has a literal \"://\" appended; host/path/query/fragment stay empty and is_valid()=0.",
    "evidence": "(\"http://h:80/p?q#f\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> 1=\"http://h:80/p?q#f\", 2..6 empty, 7=\"http://h:80/p?q#f://\", 8=0. Also (\"HTTP://\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> 7=\"http://://h:80/\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "PATH normalization (the only structural mutation): if path is non-empty and path[0] != '/', a single '/' is prepended. Empty path stays empty. No other rewriting (no dot-segment collapsing, no '//' collapsing, no trimming, no percent re-coding).",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"h\",\"-1\",\"relative/p\",\"\",\"\") -> 4=\"/relative/p\"; (\"mailto\",\"\",\"-1\",\"user@example.com\",\"\",\"\") -> 4=\"/user@example.com\", 7=\"mailto:///user@example.com\"; (\"http\",\"h\",\"-1\",\"?q\",\"\",\"\") -> 4=\"/?q\"; (\"http\",\"h\",\"-1\",\"#f\",\"\",\"\") -> 4=\"/#f\"; (\"http\",\"h\",\"-1\",\" /a\",\"\",\"\") -> 4=\"/ /a\"; (\"http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/a//b/../c\",\"\",\"\") -> 4=\"/a//b/../c\" unchanged; (\"http\",\"h\",\"-1\",\"//\",\"\",\"\") -> 4=\"//\" unchanged"
  },
  {
    "rule": "SCHEME is ASCII-lowercased byte-wise, with no trimming, no validation, and no character filtering. HOST is never case-folded. Query/fragment never case-folded.",
    "evidence": "(\"HTTP\",\"EXAMPLE.COM\",\"8080\",\"/A/B\",\"Q=V\",\"FRAG\") -> 1=\"http\", 2=\"EXAMPLE.COM\", 5=\"Q=V\", 6=\"FRAG\"; (\" HTTP \",\"h\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> 1=\" http \", 7=\" http ://h:80/\"; (\"HTTP:\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> 1=\"http:\", 7=\"http:://h:80/\"; (\"ÄÖÜ\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> 1=\"ÄÖÜ\" (non-ASCII untouched); (\"1http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> 8=1 (no scheme validation)"
  },
  {
    "rule": "QUERY and FRAGMENT are stored byte-verbatim and prefixed with a literal '?' / '#' only when non-empty — a leading '?' in query or leading '#' in fragment is duplicated, and a '#' inside query is emitted raw.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/\",\"?q\",\"\") -> 7=\"http://h:80/??q\"; (\"http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"?x\") -> 7=\"http://h:80/#?x\"; (\"HTTPS\",\"EXAMPLE.com\",\"443\",\"/a/../b%20c\",\"b=2&a=1\",\"#top\") -> 7=\"https://EXAMPLE.com:443/a/../b%20c?b=2&a=1##top\"; (\"http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/\",\"q#f\",\"\") -> 5=\"q#f\", 6=\"\", 7=\"http://h:80/?q#f\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "is_valid() == (!host.empty() || !path.empty()) — evaluated after the path '/' -prepend. It is completely independent of scheme, port, query and fragment.",
    "evidence": "Truth table (port=-1): (http,hh,/p)=1 (http,hh,\"\")=1 (http,\"\",/p)=1 (http,\"\",\"\")=0 (\"\",hh,/p)=1 (\"\",hh,\"\")=1 (\"\",\"\",/p)=1 (\"\",\"\",\"\")=0. Same table with query=\"q\" fragment=\"f\" gives identical validity, e.g. (\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"q\",\"f\") -> 7=\"http://?q#f\", 8=0. (\"\",\" \",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> 8=1 (a single space host is \"valid\"). (\"http\",\"h\",\"70000\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> 3=\"-1\", 8=1 (bad port does not invalidate)."
  },
  {
    "rule": "PORT: value = std::stoi(argv[3]) (leading whitespace/sign allowed, stops at first non-digit); then it is kept only if 0 <= v <= 65535, otherwise it is stored as -1. port() prints the stored value; to_string() includes \":port\" iff port >= 0 (so 0 IS printed).",
    "evidence": "Sweep host=h path=/: -1->-1 \"http://h/\"; 0->0 \"http://h:0/\"; 1->1; 65534->65534; 65535->65535 \"http://h:65535/\"; 65536->-1 \"http://h/\"; 65537->-1; 100000->-1; 2147483647->-1; -0->0. Also \"80abc\"->80, \" 80\"->80, \"+80\"->80, \"007\"->7, \"0x50\"->0, \"1e3\"->1, \"80.5\"->80, \"-2\"->-1, \"-100\"->-1, \"-2147483648\"->-1"
  },
  {
    "rule": "PORT parse failures are fatal (uncaught exception -> SIGABRT, exit 134, nothing on stdout): non-numeric argv[3] and EMPTY argv[3] throw std::invalid_argument; argv[3] numerically above INT_MAX throws std::out_of_range.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"h\",\"abc\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> exit=134, stderr \"terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::invalid_argument'  what():  stoi\"; (\"http\",\"h\",\"\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> exit=134 same message; (\"http\",\"h\",\"2147483648\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> exit=134, stderr \"...std::out_of_range  what():  stoi\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "IDEMPOTENCE / round-trip: the component normalization is a fixed point. Re-feeding the printed components (lines 1-6) as the 6 args always reproduces byte-identical 8-line output. 59 round-trips run (23 targeted + 36 fuzz grid), 0 mismatches.",
    "evidence": "rt(\"HTTPS\",\"EXAMPLE.com\",\"443\",\"/a/../b%20c\",\"b=2&a=1\",\"#top\"): pass1 comps=[https|EXAMPLE.com|443|/a/../b%20c|b=2&a=1|#top] ts=[https://EXAMPLE.com:443/a/../b%20c?b=2&a=1##top]; pass2 identical, \"IDEMPOTENT: yes\". Fuzz grid over 6 schemes x 6 hosts x ports/paths/queries/fragments: \"checked=36 nonidempotent=0\". Notably (\"http\",\"h\",\"70000\",\"/\") -> port -1 -> re-feed \"-1\" -> same; (\"http\",\"h\",\"80abc\",\"/\") -> 80 -> re-feed \"80\" -> same; (\"http\",\"h\",\"-1\",\"relative/p\") -> \"/relative/p\" -> re-feed -> same"
  },
  {
    "rule": "to_string() is NOT injective (string round-trip is lossy): distinct component tuples produce identical to_string() output, so re-parsing the string could not recover the components.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/p?x=1#f\",\"\",\"\") -> 7=\"http://h:80/p?x=1#f\" while (\"http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/p\",\"x=1\",\"f\") -> 7=\"http://h:80/p?x=1#f\" — same line 7, different lines 4/5/6. Likewise (\"http\",\"h:9090\",\"-1\",\"/x\",\"\",\"\") vs (\"http\",\"h\",\"9090\",\"/x\",\"\",\"\") both -> \"http://h:9090/x\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "Fields are passed through byte-exactly with no length limit and no escaping: embedded TAB survives, and an embedded NEWLINE in any field shifts the stdout line count above 8 (line-based parsers break).",
    "evidence": "300-'a' host/path/query/fragment: line2 len=300, line4 len=301 (leading '/' added), line7 len=1213, line8=\"1\" — no truncation. host=$'a\\nb' -> stdout has 10 lines: 1=http, 2=a, 3=b, 4=80, 5=/, 6=, 7=, 8=\"http://a\", 9=\"b:80/\", 10=1. query=$'a\\tb' -> 5=\"a<TAB>b\", 7=\"http://h:80/?a<TAB>b\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "Argument count: fewer than 6 positional args -> immediate exit 1 with empty stdout and empty stderr (no parsing at all). More than 6 -> extra args are silently ignored (only argv[1..6] used).",
    "evidence": "0,1,5 args -> exit=1, out_lines=0, stderr=[]; (\"http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\",\"EXTRA\",\"MORE\") -> exit=0 and the normal 8 lines ending 7=\"http://h:80/\", 8=1; 7 args of \"a\" -> exit=134 because argv[3]=\"a\" hits stoi (proof the 7th arg is reached only as ignored trailing data)"
  },
  {
    "rule": "No percent-encoding, decoding, or delimiter escaping is ever applied to any field on output.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"h\",\"80\",\"/%2Fa%20b\",\"k=%26v\",\"%23f\") -> 4=\"/%2Fa%20b\", 5=\"k=%26v\", 6=\"%23f\", 7=\"http://h:80/%2Fa%20b?k=%26v#%23f\" — bytes unchanged"
  }
]

STATED UNCERTAINTIES (resolve these definitively by experiment):
[
  "No string-parsing entry point is reachable through this CLI (all 6 args are components), so I could not test whether a from_string()/parse() function exists that WOULD split host:port, userinfo, or path?query#fragment. All 'no re-split' rules are statements about the from_components path only.",
  "Idempotence was verified at the COMPONENT level (feed lines 1-6 back in). I could not verify string-level round-trip (feed line 7 back in) because there is no way to hand the binary a whole URL string; the observed to_string() is provably non-injective, so a string round-trip could not be lossless in general.",
  "Scheme lowercasing looked byte-wise ASCII-only (ÄÖÜ unchanged, run under the container's default locale). Behavior under a different LC_ALL/LC_CTYPE was not tested.",
  "Exact stoi acceptance grammar was inferred from samples (\" 80\"->80, \"+80\"->80, \"0x50\"->0, \"1e3\"->1, \"007\"->7); I did not enumerate every whitespace form (e.g. leading tab/newline) or unusual sign combinations.",
  "Whether the port clamp is written as `if (p < 0 || p > 65535) p = -1` versus an unsigned-range check cannot be distinguished; both fit all observations (every out-of-range input, positive or negative, yields exactly -1).",
  "The <6-arg case prints nothing at all to stdout or stderr and returns 1; I could not determine whether a usage message goes somewhere else (e.g. it may simply be absent).",
  "Fields containing NUL bytes could not be tested (shell/execve limitation).",
  "Not tested: extremely large inputs near ARG_MAX, or whether any allocation failure path changes behavior."
]

Then write finalSpec: complete, unambiguous pseudocode for this dimension that a Rust implementer can follow with zero guesswork. Include the exact literal outputs for any case where the behavior is surprising.
